course: myths
lesson: 34
title: Teachers who listen, teachers who get cross — and when the teacher is right after all
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A pupil once told Mr Ellis, "Sir, you're wrong, tomatoes are vegetables!" He wasn't wrong. The pupil checked, came back and said "Sorry, sir, you were right", which took more courage than the first time.
explain: Teachers are often right, so check before you challenge. And some questions, like whether the Nile or the Amazon is longest, have no single answer yet, and a good teacher says so.
explain: The best teachers say "I'm not sure, let's all find out", or come back a week later with "I looked it up, you were right". When they do, thank them.
explain: When a pupil quietly told Mr Owen after class that Napoleon wasn't tiny, he thanked them for not saying it in front of everyone.
explain: A busy or flustered teacher who says "sit down" isn't your enemy: pick a better moment. And not every grown-up needs correcting, like a dinner lady who just wants gum in the bin.
fact: Some sources list the Nile as the longest river and others the Amazon; it depends on where you say each river starts and ends, and experts still disagree.
fact: Botanists call tomatoes fruits because they grow from the flower and hold seeds.
fact: The rhyme "divorced, beheaded, died, divorced, beheaded, survived" helps people remember Henry VIII's six wives, though historians point out his first and fourth marriages were annulled (cancelled as if they had never properly counted), which is not quite a divorce in today's sense.
fact: Since 2006 Pluto has been called a dwarf planet.
fact: The Great Wall of China is practically impossible to see with the naked eye from orbit (circling the Earth in space), astronauts report.
fact: The "tongue map", with sweet only on the tip and bitter only at the back, is wrong; all parts of the tongue with taste buds can taste all basic tastes.
fact: Whether hot water freezes faster than cold is still an open question in science.
fact: Some websites are reliable, like those of NASA, museums or the NHS, and many are not.
fact: Swallowed chewing gum does not stay for seven years; it passes through the body in days, though swallowing lots is not good.
fact: Napoleon was about average height for a Frenchman of his time; the "short Napoleon" story partly came from mixed-up French and English inches and from cartoons.
fact: Correcting a teacher privately instead of in front of everyone is kind and is usually welcomed.
fact: Checking before you challenge saves you from being wrong in front of everyone.
exercise: Which is the longest river in the world? | Experts still disagree between the Nile and the Amazon | Definitely the Amazon, because all the sources agree on it | Definitely the Nile, all sources agree | The Mississippi, by a long way
exercise: Why can't experts agree on the longest river? | It depends where you say each river starts and ends | Nobody has ever properly measured either of the rivers | The rivers change length every day | Maps are not allowed to show rivers
exercise: Mr Ellis says tomatoes are fruit. Was he right? | Yes, botanists call them fruit | No, tomatoes are vegetables only | No, tomatoes are nuts | Yes, but only cherry tomatoes
exercise: What should you do before telling a teacher they're wrong? | Check that you're right, with a good source | Get all your friends to agree with you first | Say it loudly so you don't lose your nerve | Wait until the teacher is busy
exercise: Is "divorced" exactly right for Henry VIII's first wife? | Not quite; historians say the marriage was annulled | Yes, it's exactly right in every way | No, she was beheaded | No, she outlived Henry
exercise: Mrs Robinson says "We haven't got time for this. Sit down." What's best? | Sit down and ask at a better time | Keep on arguing until she finally listens | Walk straight out of the class in a huff | Shout the fact across the room at her
exercise: Can the Great Wall be seen with the naked eye from orbit? | Practically no, astronauts say | Yes, easily, it's the only thing you see | Yes, but only at night | Only from the Moon
exercise: Mr Hughes comes back after a week: "You were right about the Great Wall." What do you say? | Thanks for checking, sir | Told you so, sir | I knew you were wrong all along | Can I have a merit for that?
exercise: Is the tongue map right? | No, all parts of the tongue can taste every basic taste | Yes, sweet is only on the tip | Yes, bitter is only at the back | No, the tongue can only taste salt
exercise: A teacher says "the textbook says it" about the tongue map. How could you check? | Do a careful taste test at home | Argue that textbooks are always wrong | Rip the page out of the book | Tell everyone the teacher is lying
exercise: Mr Brown says "I'm not sure, let's all find out". What kind of answer is that? | The best kind, it's real science | A bad one, because teachers must know everything | A lazy one, he's just trying to avoid work | A trick to stop the lesson for a while
exercise: Mrs Lee says "the internet is full of rubbish". What's the best reply? | You're right; this one is from NASA, is that OK? | No, everything online is true | Then let's never use the internet | Then your books are rubbish too
exercise: Does swallowed gum stay inside you for seven years? | No, it passes through in days | Yes, exactly seven years, then it comes out | Yes, it stays inside you forever | No, it melts away while still in your mouth
exercise: The dinner lady says gum stays seven years. What's wise? | Say "OK" and put the gum in the bin | Give her a long lecture all about digestion | Tell the head teacher that she is wrong | Swallow the gum right away to prove her wrong
exercise: How tall was Napoleon? | About average for a Frenchman of his time | Tiny, much smaller than anyone else in his army | Very tall, over two metres | Nobody knows, there are no records
exercise: Why did Mr Owen thank the pupil? | They told him privately, not in front of everyone | They agreed with absolutely everything he said in class | They brought him a present | They stayed quiet about the mistake
test: "Miss you're WRONG, the Amazon is longest!!" What's the wisest response from a teacher? | It's argued, both are listed; let's look at why | You're wrong, sit down | You're right, the Nile is a short river | Rivers aren't on the curriculum
test: A pupil was sure tomatoes are veg and told Mr Ellis off. After checking, what should the pupil do? | Say "Sorry, sir, you were right" | Pretend it never happened | Find another fact to prove him wrong | Tell everyone the botany book is wrong
test: "divorced beheaded died, divorced beheaded survived — sir that's wrong" Is it? | It's a good memory rhyme, but some marriages were annulled | It's totally wrong, he had eight wives | It's completely right in every detail | He only had one wife really
test: What would happen if you tested the tongue map with sugar on the back of your tongue? | You'd taste the sweetness there too | You'd taste nothing at all on that spot | You'd taste salt instead of anything sweet | Your tongue would go numb at the tip
test: Mr Hughes says he checked and you were right. Why is he a good role model? | He admitted a mistake after checking | He never makes any mistakes about anything | He let you win just to be nice to you | He stopped teaching about the Great Wall
test: "nah gum stays in u 7 yrs, dinner lady said" What's true? | It passes through in days, but don't swallow lots | It's 7 years exactly, she knows | It turns into a ball in your heart | It stays forever and grows
test: Mrs Robinson is flustered and says "sit down" when you ask about Pluto. What's the best plan? | Ask at hometime if you can talk another day | Refuse to sit until she answers | Ask the whole class to have a vote on Pluto | Say she doesn't know about Pluto
test: Which website is best to show a teacher who distrusts the internet? | NASA's own page | A random forum post | A meme account | A video with no name on it
test: Mr Brown's class tests whether hot water freezes faster and gets mixed results. What does that show? | Real science can have no clear answer yet | The class did it wrong | Hot water never freezes | Mr Brown should have known the answer
test: The cartoon Napoleon is tiny and stamps his feet. What's the truth? | He was about average height for his time | He was the shortest man in France | He was a giant, the cartoons are right | He was short because he was always angry
test: You want to tell Mr Owen he's wrong about Napoleon. Which way is kindest? | Quietly, after class, with an article | Loudly, with the whole class listening | In a note signed "everyone" | By telling another teacher first
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the present state of knowledge concerning the longest river on Earth? | It remains disputed between the Nile and the Amazon | It is conclusively established as the Amazon | It is conclusively established as the Nile | It has never been the subject of any measurement at all
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the so-called tongue map. | It is erroneous, since all regions bearing taste buds detect every basic taste | It is accurate in every particular | It is accurate solely in respect of sweetness | It is accurate solely in respect of bitterness
exam: It is given that chewing gum has been swallowed. Determine the period within which it ordinarily passes through the body. | Within a matter of days | After a period of seven years | Never | After a period of seven months
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a teacher may welcome a correction delivered in private. | It avoids causing embarrassment before the class | It ensures that the pupil is invariably proved right | It obliges the teacher to award a merit | It removes any need for evidence
